    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "What changed",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 4,
        "w": 9,
        "h": 18
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Spring \u2192 Summer: what changed\n\n**1. The division scored a lot more in Summer.**\nLeague run rate 5.76 \u2192 7.35 (+28%); average score 104 \u2192 133.\nThat is why this page compares ranks, not raw numbers.\n\n**2. Our batting stood still while the league moved.**\nRun rate 7.20 \u2192 7.12 and average score 133 \u2192 133.\nThat made us the best batting side in Spring (1st of 16) and 11th in Summer.\n\n**3. The middle overs are the biggest change.**\nWickets lost in overs 7-15: 2.25 \u2192 4.22 (1st \u2192 11th).\nDeath-overs scoring fell too: 9.53 \u2192 7.94 (1st \u2192 11th).\n\n**4. The bowling improved, relative to the division.**\nEconomy rank 15th \u2192 8th, dot balls 16th \u2192 5th, wickets per match 12th \u2192 4th.\n\n**5. Both knockout exits were chases that ended all out.**\nSpring QF: 102 all out chasing 120. Summer QF: 133 all out chasing 161.\nWe were bowled out once in Spring and 4 times in Summer.\n\n**6. More rotation.** Batters used: 22 \u2192 26.\n\n**7. Same result both times:** quarter-finalists (league 5-2 \u2192 5-3).\n\n<small>11 of the 16 Div A teams played both seasons. Spring excludes 5 matches with no result. Source: CricClubs.</small>"
      },
      "id": 5
    },
